# Auto detect text files and perform LF normalization
* text=auto

# --- Working-tree line endings, pinned (2026-07-21) -------------------------------------------
#
# `text=auto` alone normalises to LF **on commit only**. The checkout side stays platform
# dependent, so on Windows (`core.autocrlf`) a `git checkout` can hand the working tree CRLF
# while git still reports it clean — the difference is invisible to `git status` precisely
# because normalisation happens at commit time.
#
# That split cost three separate agents on 2026-07-21, after a `git checkout -- apps/web`
# rewrote that subtree as CRLF while the rest of the repo stayed LF:
#   * two replay scripts whose exact-match anchors silently stopped matching, on files whose
#     content was byte-identical to HEAD;
#   * `apps/web/src/app/motion.test.ts`, whose regex ends `\n\}\n` and therefore cannot match
#     a block closing `}\r\n`. Exactly 1 test out of 1655 noticed.
#
# Pinning `eol` makes checkout deterministic on every platform, so any assertion that reads a
# file's raw bytes is correct by construction rather than by luck of the developer's OS.
*.ts   text eol=lf
*.tsx  text eol=lf
*.js   text eol=lf
*.mjs  text eol=lf
*.cjs  text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.css  text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.rs   text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.sql  text eol=lf
*.sh   text eol=lf

# PEM key material is read by `include_str!` and compared against the canonical form the
# normaliser emits, which is always LF. Left unpinned, a Windows runner (`core.autocrlf=true`,
# the Git-for-Windows default the GitHub Actions images ship) checks these out as CRLF and the
# armour line the assertion slices out carries a trailing `\r` — one test out of 1614 failed on
# `Rust (windows-latest)` and nowhere else, on a tree byte-identical to the green Linux one.
#
# Deliberately NOT extended to `*.xml`, `*.csv` or `*.der`: those fixtures carry committed CRLF
# and lone-CR bytes ON PURPOSE — `crates/chancela-xades/tests/fixtures/c14n/v15_line_ending_
# normalization.in.xml` is a canonicalization vector whose whole subject is the `\r\n` in it, and
# an `eol=lf` pin would silently rewrite the thing under test.
*.pem  text eol=lf

# Windows batch files are the one genuine exception: `cmd.exe` can mis-parse LF-terminated
# scripts, so these stay CRLF deliberately rather than by platform accident.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
